.TH KEYBIND-FIND 1 2026-10-27 "keybind-find 0.1.0-alpha.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
keybind-find \- search the keybindings for the focused window and run the one you pick
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keybind\-find
.RB [ \-\-print ]
.br
.B keybind\-find dump
.RI [ provider ]
.br
.B keybind\-find keys
.br
.B keybind\-find plugin list
.br
.B keybind\-find plugin add
.I name
.br
.B keybind\-find check\-plugin
.IR name | path
.RB [ \-\-input
.IR file ]...
.RB [ \-\-class
.IR class ]
.RB [ \-\-program
.IR program ]
.RB [ \-\-title
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B keybind\-find
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B keybind\-find
lists the keyboard shortcuts that apply to the focused window: the window manager's (xmonad, sway), the focused
app's (alacritty, and nvim, herdr or tmux running in it; Vimium and web-app sheets in a browser), espanso's matches,
and any sheets you wrote.
The lists are read from the programs' own configuration.
Pick one and keybind-find sends its keys to the window that had focus, or types the espanso trigger.
It is meant to be bound to a hotkey, such as
.BR Super+/ .
.PP
On X11 the list opens in
.BR fzf (1)
in an
.BR alacritty (1)
window (float it in your window manager by its class,
.BR keybind\-find ),
in key search: press a binding's keys to narrow the list, Enter to run it, Escape to search by text, and Escape
again to close the list.
Key search needs fzf 0.66 or newer and
.BR xmodmap (1);
without them the list opens in text search and its header says why.
On Wayland it opens in
.BR fuzzel (1).
Wayland support is sway's: under another compositor only XWayland windows are read.
On X11
without fzf or alacritty it opens in
.BR dmenu (1).
.PP
A provider that fails prints one line to standard error and is left out; the others still show.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B keybind\-find
Show the list and send the binding picked.
Cancelling the list sends nothing.
.TP
.B keybind\-find \-\-print
The same flow, but print the commands that would send the keys instead of running them.
Nothing is logged.
.TP
\fBkeybind\-find dump\fR [\fIprovider\fR]
Print every sheet for the focused window as a JSON array, or with
.IR provider ,
that provider's sheet: one of
.BR xmonad ,
.BR espanso ,
.BR nvim ,
.BR herdr ,
.BR sway ,
.BR alacritty ,
.BR sheets ,
.BR tmux ,
.BR vimium .
.TP
.B keybind\-find keys
Grab the keyboard and print each key pressed, in keybind-find's key notation, until Escape or 30 seconds with no
key (X11 only).
.TP
.B keybind\-find plugin list
List the plugins installed: executables named
.BI keybind\-find\- name
on
.BR PATH ,
and executables in
.IR ~/.config/keybind-find/plugins/ .
Each line says whether a
.B [[plugin]]
stanza in the config runs it, then what its manifest says (the plugin's answer to
.BR \-\-manifest ).
Super+/ runs only the plugins a stanza names.
.TP
\fBkeybind\-find plugin add\fR \fIname\fR
Append a commented
.B [[plugin]]
stanza for the installed plugin
.I name
to the config, with the plugin's path as its program and its default inputs and match, and print it.
The config is created if missing and otherwise only appended to; a config that already has a stanza of that name or
for that program, isn't valid TOML, or has a
.B plugin
key that isn't
.B [[plugin]]
stanzas, is refused and left as it is.
.TP
\fBkeybind\-find check\-plugin\fR \fIname\fR|\fIpath\fR
Check a plugin, for its author: ask it for its manifest, run it as Super+/ would, and print one line per problem
with where it is (category, index and key), then a summary.
It checks that the manifest is valid JSON of version 1 with the fields it needs (a plugin that refuses
.B \-\-manifest
by exiting non-zero is allowed, and noted); that the response is valid JSON of
version 1 in DMS's shape, with every key in the key notation, no empty descriptions, no key twice in one category
and scope, and
.B when
values of the right type; the exit status (and stderr, when it fails); and each run time against the 3 s limit.
The plugin is a path (with a
.BR / ),
a configured stanza's name, or an installed plugin's name.
It is sent the files given with
.B \-\-input
(repeatable), else its stanza's inputs, else its manifest's default inputs, and a window with the
.BR \-\-class ,
.B \-\-program
and
.B \-\-title
given; a note says when its match would not run it in that window.
.B \-\-json
prints the report as JSON instead, for agents and CI.
Exits 0 only when there are no problems.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage line.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KEYBIND_FIND_PICKER
A dmenu-style program to use as the picker instead of fzf, fuzzel or dmenu: it reads one line per binding on
standard input and prints the chosen one.
The value is split on whitespace and run without a shell.
Empty counts as unset.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where
.I keybind\-find/
holds the config, sheets and Vimium backup (default
.IR ~/.config ),
and where sway, alacritty and herdr's configs are looked for.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the usage log lives (default
.IR ~/.local/state ).
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where keybind-find looks for the keymap xmonad writes (default
.IR ~/.cache );
it must match xmonad's own environment.
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Set and non-empty: Wayland (fuzzel, and
.BR wtype (1)
to send keys).
.TP
.B SWAYSOCK
Set and non-empty together with
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY :
sway, read with
.BR swaymsg (1).
.TP
.B HERDR_CONFIG_PATH
herdr's
.I config.toml
when set, as herdr itself reads it.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the fzf picker keeps its list and sockets while open (else the temporary directory).
.TP
.BR FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS ", " FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS_FILE
Ignored: the picker's fzf runs without them.
.PP
A relative XDG directory is ignored, as the XDG Base Directory specification says.
.SH FILES
Paths are shown with their defaults; the XDG variables above move them.
.TP
.I ~/.config/keybind-find/config.toml
Rebinds keybind-find's own keys in the fzf picker (the
.B [keys]
table), and declares plugins
.RB ( [[plugin]]
stanzas).
A config with an unknown field or clashing keys is refused as a whole: every key keeps its default, and the
picker's header says why.
.TP
.I ~/.config/keybind-find/sheets/
Your own sheets: JSON files matched by window class (and optionally title), and executables named after a window class that print
one.
.TP
.I ~/.config/keybind-find/vimium-options.json
A Vimium backup, for your Vimium remaps.
.TP
.I ~/.config/keybind-find/plugins/
Plugins that aren't on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/keybind-find/manifests.json
The plugins' manifests, kept until a plugin's file changes, so a run doesn't ask each plugin again.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/keybind-find/usage.tsv
The usage log: one line per pick, with the time, provider and key.
Delete it to reset the ranking.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/keybind-find/xmonad.keys
xmonad's keymap, written by xmonad when its keybind-find key is pressed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a cancelled list.
.TP
.B 1
An error, such as nothing to show, an unknown provider for
.BR dump ,
or a pick that couldn't be sent.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: unknown arguments, or arguments that aren't UTF-8.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fzf (1),
.BR alacritty (1),
.BR fuzzel (1),
.BR dmenu (1),
.BR xdotool (1),
.BR wtype (1),
.BR xmonad (1),
.BR sway (1)
.PP
The README and
.I docs/
in the source tree describe sheets, the config and each provider in full.
